## Cut down on salt

Most salt is hidden in food we buy, such as bread, ready meals, soups, sauces and processed meat.

- check **food labels**: more than 1.5g of salt per 100g is high
- **cook from scratch** when you can
- use **herbs, spices, lemon or garlic** for flavour instead of salt
- keep the salt pot **off the table**

## Eat well

Eat plenty of **fruit, vegetables and wholegrains**, and choose **low-fat dairy**. Cutting down on sugary and fatty foods also helps you keep to a healthy weight.

## Move more

Any activity that makes you a little out of breath helps, such as brisk walking, cycling or swimming. Aim for **150 minutes a week**, in chunks of 10 minutes or more if that is easier. Check with your GP first if your blood pressure is very high.

## Alcohol and smoking

- keep to **14 units a week or less**, spread over 3 or more days
- **stopping smoking** is one of the best things you can do for your heart. Your local stop smoking service can help, for free

## Stress and sleep

Stress does not cause long-term high blood pressure on its own, but it can lead to habits that do. Find ways to relax that work for you, and aim for **7 to 9 hours of sleep**. If you snore loudly and feel tired in the day, ask your GP about sleep apnoea, which can raise blood pressure.

## Keep track

Keep a note of your home readings and take them to your reviews. Many people find it motivating to see their numbers come down.
